WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.808 --> 00:00:13.532
Hi, Little Fox readers.

2
00:00:14.373 --> 00:00:14.993
It's Alex.

3
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:20.155
I'm back to tell you about an awesome landmark that we just learned about in Mr.

4
00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:22.876
Fay's class: the Amazon River.

5
00:00:23.777 --> 00:00:24.817
Have you ever heard of it?

6
00:00:25.657 --> 00:00:27.938
If not, get ready to be impressed!

7
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:36.055
Here are some of the coolest things about it: First, the Amazon is the longest river in South

8
00:00:36.115 --> 00:00:41.059
America and the biggest river in the world because it holds the most water.

9
00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:49.366
Second, the Earth's most unique rain forest lies in the Amazon's basin, which is the land around a

10
00:00:49.426 --> 00:00:55.692
river. And the third coolest thing about it is Wait, I'm going to save that one for last!

11
00:00:57.216 --> 00:01:03.300
The Amazon River runs from the Andes Mountains in Peru to the coast of the Atlantic Ocean in

12
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.081
Brazil. Mr.

13
00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:11.785
Fay says river channels change all the time, so it's hard to figure out the Amazon's exact length.

14
00:01:12.766 --> 00:01:17.909
But it's usually considered to be about 6,400 kilometers long.

15
00:01:18.769 --> 00:01:22.912
It carries about one-fifth of all the river water on Earth.

16
00:01:23.836 --> 00:01:30.658
That's more water than the Nile, Mississippi, Yangtze, and Ganges Rivers combined!

17
00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.939
How did it get to be so huge?

18
00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:41.421
Well, the Amazon drains an area that is nearly as big as the United States, and in its

19
00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:45.622
southern parts, the rainy season can last nearly half the year.

20
00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:48.123
In class Mr.

21
00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:51.544
Fay showed us a book about how the Amazon got its name.

22
00:01:52.596 --> 00:01:54.257
It's a pretty interesting story.

23
00:01:55.217 --> 00:02:03.041
Although South American natives have lived on the shores of the Amazon for around 12,000 years, Europeans didn't

24
00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:05.603
discover it until about 500 years ago.

25
00:02:06.483 --> 00:02:14.427
In 1541 a Spanish explorer named Francisco de Orellana was sent to look for land to conquer, and

26
00:02:14.487 --> 00:02:15.968
he ended up on the Amazon.

27
00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:24.741
His 3,017-kilometer journey on the river made him the first European to navigate its dangerous waters.

28
00:02:25.682 --> 00:02:28.023
But it was no easy expedition.

29
00:02:29.384 --> 00:02:36.088
Apparently Orellana and his men were attacked by native female warriors who lived along the river.

30
00:02:37.029 --> 00:02:41.732
These women were fierce, fighting off Orellana's party with bows and arrows.

31
00:02:42.788 --> 00:02:46.971
According to Orellana each woman had the strength of ten men!

32
00:02:47.912 --> 00:02:54.256
He was so impressed by them that he called them the Amazons after the female warriors in Greek

33
00:02:54.296 --> 00:03:01.201
mythology. Later the same name was given to the mighty river where Orellana had encountered these women.

34
00:03:02.082 --> 00:03:10.087
It wasn't until much later, though, that people discovered all the natural resources along the Amazon River basin—like

35
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:15.273
fish, Brazil nuts, and animal skins—and flocked to it.

36
00:03:16.073 --> 00:03:23.315
In the mid-1800s a rubber boom brought the first huge wave of North Americans and Europeans to the

37
00:03:23.455 --> 00:03:27.777
area. Did you know that rubber comes from trees?

38
00:03:28.757 --> 00:03:35.239
People put taps in the tree trunks to extract latex, the same way people collect maple syrup from

39
00:03:35.299 --> 00:03:36.099
maple trees.

40
00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:39.760
Then the latex is turned into rubber.

41
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.042
And the good thing is that extracting latex doesn't hurt the tree at all.

42
00:03:45.922 --> 00:03:51.785
Anyway, the rubber tree is one of the most common trees in the Amazon rain forest, and the

43
00:03:51.865 --> 00:03:56.207
North Americans and Europeans were very excited when they discovered it.

44
00:03:57.087 --> 00:04:03.470
They saw that they could make waterproof tires, shoes, and other things with this new material.

45
00:04:04.411 --> 00:04:11.427
Unfortunately, in their quest for rubber, they killed many of the natives and cut down lots of trees

46
00:04:11.487 --> 00:04:12.889
to power their steamboats.

47
00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:17.272
This was the beginning of the destruction of the Amazon.

48
00:04:18.553 --> 00:04:25.239
Today huge sections of the Amazon rain forest are being cleared for logging and cattle ranching.

49
00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.042
This harms both the rain forest and the river.

50
00:04:29.883 --> 00:04:35.868
When there are no trees to shade the ground, the land and river channels dry up more quickly.

51
00:04:36.784 --> 00:04:41.906
Areas that were once rich with plant and animal life are now barren deserts.

52
00:04:42.887 --> 00:04:44.808
I couldn't believe it when I read that!

53
00:04:45.828 --> 00:04:50.410
Luckily, a lot of organizations are working hard to preserve what's left.

54
00:04:51.451 --> 00:04:57.354
Scientists think that more than a third of the world's plant and animal species live in the Amazon

55
00:04:57.414 --> 00:04:58.134
River basin.

56
00:04:59.014 --> 00:05:02.356
Can you imagine how horrible it would be if they disappeared?

57
00:05:03.804 --> 00:05:09.608
We learned about lots of different plants and animals that live in the rain forest, but I think

58
00:05:09.688 --> 00:05:12.591
the really big animals are the most interesting.

59
00:05:13.511 --> 00:05:20.857
There's the anaconda, the world's largest snake, which can grow to be over ten meters long.

60
00:05:21.797 --> 00:05:25.120
But it isn't the only giant predator lurking in the river.

61
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.684
There's also the black caiman, a six-meter long crocodile.

62
00:05:31.604 --> 00:05:36.749
The black caiman is endangered because it was once intensely hunted for its skin.

63
00:05:37.749 --> 00:05:43.214
Now its numbers are slowly on the rise because anti-hunting laws were put in place.

64
00:05:44.235 --> 00:05:47.958
In the meantime I bet the animals it preys on are happy!

65
00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:54.784
These include the capybara, a huge rodent, and the piranha, a vicious fish.

66
00:05:56.332 --> 00:06:01.977
By now you probably want to know how you can visit the Amazon River and the amazing rain

67
00:06:02.057 --> 00:06:03.078
forest around it.

68
00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:08.203
According to experts the best way is definitely by boat.

69
00:06:09.224 --> 00:06:14.309
Lots of cargo ships still ride down the river, and you can catch a ride on one of

70
00:06:14.329 --> 00:06:16.891
those and sleep in a hammock on the deck.

71
00:06:17.892 --> 00:06:20.674
But I'm sure tour boats would be more comfortable.

72
00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:26.781
If I went, I would go on a week-long tour that starts in Iquitos, Peru.

73
00:06:27.722 --> 00:06:34.363
Iquitos is near the Andes Mountains, and it's the largest city in South America that can't be reached

74
00:06:34.444 --> 00:06:35.144
by roads.

75
00:06:36.144 --> 00:06:38.065
That sounds pretty awesome to me.

76
00:06:38.985 --> 00:06:44.146
But if you don't like boats, you can stay at one of the hotels along the riverbank.

77
00:06:45.607 --> 00:06:49.568
Now are you ready to learn the third coolest thing about the Amazon?

78
00:06:50.556 --> 00:06:56.761
It's the Great River Amazon Raft Race, the longest raft race in the world!

79
00:06:57.742 --> 00:07:02.506
Teams from all over the globe paddle down the river on homemade, wooden rafts.

80
00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:08.671
The race is 180 kilometers long and lasts for three days!

81
00:07:09.772 --> 00:07:12.354
I hope I get to compete in the raft race one day.

82
00:07:12.374 --> 00:07:19.439
I can already picture myself swatting mosquitoes and wiping sweat off my face as I paddle down the

83
00:07:19.559 --> 00:07:20.040
Amazon!
